WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.567 --> 00:00:02.901
-Welcome, welcome back.
We have so much to talk about.

00:00:02.901 --> 00:00:04.400
How's the family, by the way,
first of all?

00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:08.834
-Family, amazing.
My three daughters thriving.

00:00:08.834 --> 00:00:11.601
We came back
from a summer in Spain.

00:00:11.601 --> 00:00:13.501
-Yeah, you Zoomed
with me from Spain.

00:00:13.501 --> 00:00:14.534
Thank you for doing that,
by the way.

00:00:14.534 --> 00:00:16.000
-Yep.
-And I just got to see

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:17.667
how fun you are
with the whole fam.

00:00:17.667 --> 00:00:18.801
Here they are, actually.

00:00:18.801 --> 00:00:19.934
I have a picture of you
and the family.

00:00:19.934 --> 00:00:21.400
Oh, this is --
your daughter's graduated?

00:00:21.400 --> 00:00:23.367
-Yep.
-Yeah, look at that.

00:00:23.367 --> 00:00:25.100
Come on.
-Ines, Carlota, Lucia.

00:00:25.100 --> 00:00:29.767
I gave the speech on her
graduation -- on my youngest.

00:00:29.767 --> 00:00:31.534
-Oh, really?
At the school?

00:00:31.534 --> 00:00:33.167
-And I was so super-nervous,

00:00:33.167 --> 00:00:38.767
because my daughter kind of
introduced me to everybody.

00:00:38.767 --> 00:00:41.000
And she goes into the speech,
and she says, "Well, my dad,

00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:45.334
you may think, is a good daddy,
but he never let us order food

00:00:45.334 --> 00:00:47.300
from the kids' menu
in the restaurant."

00:00:47.300 --> 00:00:49.901
[ Laughter ]
Like, really?

00:00:49.901 --> 00:00:52.334
This was the nicest thing
you could say about me?

00:00:52.334 --> 00:00:53.601
[ Laughter ]

00:00:53.601 --> 00:00:56.167
I mean, anyway, daughters --
I love my daughters.

00:00:56.167 --> 00:00:58.868
-Yeah, of course, yeah.
I want to talk about

00:00:58.868 --> 00:01:00.934
World Central Kitchen,
because we've done --

00:01:00.934 --> 00:01:03.334
we were together in Puerto Rico.

00:01:03.334 --> 00:01:07.234
And sadly, Puerto Rico got hit
with another Hurricane now.

00:01:07.234 --> 00:01:09.801
It is rough.
But, I --

00:01:09.801 --> 00:01:11.801
What you've done
for so many people,

00:01:11.801 --> 00:01:14.934
I mean, I think the first one,
maybe, was that Bahamas

00:01:14.934 --> 00:01:17.334
or something?
-Well, Bahamas was a big one.

00:01:17.334 --> 00:01:19.501
We did over 3 million meals
with 14 islands,

00:01:19.501 --> 00:01:24.267
but we began really in Haiti
in 2010 after the earthquake.

00:01:24.267 --> 00:01:26.367
And this was very simple.
It's not me.

00:01:26.367 --> 00:01:27.968
It's the people.

00:01:27.968 --> 00:01:30.133
Yes, I go, and I tell
everybody, "Let's cook!

00:01:30.133 --> 00:01:33.334
Let's feed anybody that needs --
needs our help."

00:01:33.334 --> 00:01:35.367
And let me tell you
what happens.

00:01:35.367 --> 00:01:37.834
Everybody wants
to join this simple idea.

00:01:37.834 --> 00:01:41.067
Big problems, actually,
they have very simple solutions.

00:01:41.067 --> 00:01:42.234
Cooks, what do we do?

00:01:42.234 --> 00:01:44.300
We start cooking,
and we start feeding.

00:01:44.300 --> 00:01:46.701
You see, we can do it
when we all come together.

00:01:46.701 --> 00:01:47.968
That's what we've been doing.

00:01:47.968 --> 00:01:50.334
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:50.334 --> 00:01:52.334
-It's amazing what you're doing.
We were together --

00:01:52.334 --> 00:01:55.934
We were together in Puerto Rico,
and we were eating pastelitos.

00:01:55.934 --> 00:02:00.667
-You show up and spend there
many days doing a show,

00:02:00.667 --> 00:02:03.167
showing the beautiful people
of Puerto Rico

00:02:03.167 --> 00:02:05.100
coming back, coming together.

00:02:05.100 --> 00:02:07.767
That you took the step
to leave your studio,

00:02:07.767 --> 00:02:10.701
show up in Puerto Rico,
and give love and hope

00:02:10.701 --> 00:02:12.601
and show all America
and all the world

00:02:12.601 --> 00:02:15.100
how amazing these people are,
that was amazing.

00:02:15.100 --> 00:02:16.801
So, thank you for doing that.
Thank you.

00:02:16.801 --> 00:02:20.434
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:02:20.434 --> 00:02:23.167
-It was just --
obviously, you feel it.

00:02:23.167 --> 00:02:24.767
You feel it with the people
when you see everyone

00:02:24.767 --> 00:02:25.801
and you get there.

00:02:25.801 --> 00:02:28.033
And Puerto Rico is so beautiful.
Go visit.

00:02:28.033 --> 00:02:31.133
Tourism is such a part
of their economy,

00:02:31.133 --> 00:02:32.767
so please go support
Puerto Rico.

00:02:32.767 --> 00:02:34.701
But I remember, I ate --
I took a bite of the --

00:02:34.701 --> 00:02:37.200
and you said,
"Jimmy, you're eating it wrong."

00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:39.534
And I go, "What do you mean?"
You go, "When you eat food

00:02:39.534 --> 00:02:42.133
this good,
you have to close your eyes."

00:02:42.133 --> 00:02:43.367
[ Laughter ]

00:02:43.367 --> 00:02:45.734
-I mean,
he was not closing his eyes.

00:02:45.734 --> 00:02:46.968
[ Laughter ]

00:02:46.968 --> 00:02:48.100
-That's so funny.

00:02:48.100 --> 00:02:50.767
-He's just eating it
with his open eyes.

00:02:50.767 --> 00:02:52.601
-"His open eyes"?
Yeah.

00:02:52.601 --> 00:02:55.100
-He look like a shark.
I mean, I like sharks.

00:02:55.100 --> 00:02:56.868
Sharks are good people,
believe me.

00:02:56.868 --> 00:02:58.767
But, I mean --
-That's ridiculous.

00:02:58.767 --> 00:03:01.701
-But he's there, he's eating,
and the woman are looking like,

00:03:01.701 --> 00:03:03.400
"What's happening there?"
"I don't know."

00:03:03.400 --> 00:03:05.934
I said, "Jimmy,
can you show some emotion?"

00:03:05.934 --> 00:03:07.033
I mean --
-[ Laughs ] Come on.

00:03:07.033 --> 00:03:09.033
-Show me -- "Mmm!"

00:03:09.033 --> 00:03:10.534
You did great in the second try.

00:03:10.534 --> 00:03:11.634
-Look at you now.
[ Light laughter ]

00:03:11.634 --> 00:03:13.100
I feel like you're, like,
an actor now.

00:03:13.100 --> 00:03:14.868
'Cause now I look at you --
'cause you're in a cartoon,

00:03:14.868 --> 00:03:16.300
and now you're a big actor now.

00:03:16.300 --> 00:03:18.400
So it's gone to your head.
Look at your acting gear.

00:03:18.400 --> 00:03:20.200
You're so --
Yes, it's -- you're changing.

00:03:20.200 --> 00:03:22.400
-It's not going to my head!
They couldn't find anybody else

00:03:22.400 --> 00:03:24.534
with an accent, and they --
[ Laughter ]

00:03:24.534 --> 00:03:28.234
And they --
And they picked me.

00:03:28.234 --> 00:03:31.234
-It's called "Firebuds,"
and here you are --

00:03:31.234 --> 00:03:34.300
you're Chef Al in "Firebuds."
And I thought that was

00:03:34.300 --> 00:03:36.367
really cool to do,
just for all the kids out there.

00:03:36.367 --> 00:03:38.167
-I'm loving it.
It was great.

00:03:38.167 --> 00:03:41.334
Nobody could understand my
English, so that's fine.

00:03:41.334 --> 00:03:42.400
That's why they called me.

00:03:42.400 --> 00:03:44.868
But, listen,
I hope I'll do more.

00:03:44.868 --> 00:03:46.067
-How -- How are you doing that?

00:03:46.067 --> 00:03:47.501
You're giving all of your time.

00:03:47.501 --> 00:03:49.701
You're flying --
You're in Ukraine,

00:03:49.701 --> 00:03:51.000
you're doing all the stuff,

00:03:51.000 --> 00:03:53.968
and you're opening restaurants,
as well.

00:03:53.968 --> 00:03:55.033
-Well --

00:03:55.033 --> 00:03:57.534
-You opened one
here in New York.

00:03:57.534 --> 00:04:00.367
-At the new Ritz-Carlton hotel,
in the NoMad,

00:04:00.367 --> 00:04:02.767
I opened a restaurant
called Zaytinya, which is Greek.

00:04:02.767 --> 00:04:04.400
I opened a bar in the rooftop,

00:04:04.400 --> 00:04:06.901
has one of the best rooftops
in the history of New York.

00:04:06.901 --> 00:04:10.000
-You have another one in L.A.,
but how do you manage your time?

00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:11.133
Do you know
where you are right now?

00:04:11.133 --> 00:04:12.968
-Well, I mean...
-Do you know where --

00:04:12.968 --> 00:04:14.434
Take a guess --
Take a guess where you are.

00:04:14.434 --> 00:04:18.267
-I mean, Jimmy --
Jimmy Fallon show, New York.

00:04:18.267 --> 00:04:19.901
-Yes! You're right!
You nailed it.

00:04:19.901 --> 00:04:21.467
You nailed it, New York.
[ Applause ]

00:04:21.467 --> 00:04:22.734
-But, you know, right now,

00:04:22.734 --> 00:04:27.167
obviously, you see what
has happened with Fiona.

00:04:27.167 --> 00:04:29.434
Huge hurricane,
has affected multiple islands,

00:04:29.434 --> 00:04:31.767
multiple countries.

00:04:31.767 --> 00:04:34.467
I can be here with you now,
because the team we have

00:04:34.467 --> 00:04:36.133
at World Central Kitchen
is beyond amazing.

00:04:36.133 --> 00:04:38.434
Right now,
we are in Dominican Republic,

00:04:38.434 --> 00:04:40.300
in Puerto Rico, in Bermuda.

00:04:40.300 --> 00:04:42.234
Tuesday,
landed in Turks and Caicos.

00:04:42.234 --> 00:04:43.634
They are in Alaska.

00:04:43.634 --> 00:04:45.834
They just came from Jackson,
helping with water.

00:04:45.834 --> 00:04:47.734
We're in Pakistan
and Afghanistan.

00:04:47.734 --> 00:04:51.734
We reach 160 million meals alone
inside Ukraine.

00:04:51.734 --> 00:04:54.000
I do what I do
because the people are amazing.

00:04:54.000 --> 00:04:55.300
[ Applause ]

00:04:55.300 --> 00:04:58.601
-Do you think --
It's awesome, what you're doing.

00:04:58.601 --> 00:05:00.434
-But you know how we do it?

00:05:00.434 --> 00:05:02.634
Are you hungry?
You need help?

00:05:02.634 --> 00:05:03.901
You need food?
-Yeah.

00:05:03.901 --> 00:05:05.934
-So, okay.
This was in your greenroom.

00:05:05.934 --> 00:05:07.067
Here, food.
-Thank you so much.

00:05:07.067 --> 00:05:08.501
-Carrots.
-Yes, thank you.

00:05:08.501 --> 00:05:12.467
Did this inspire --
[ Laughter ]

00:05:12.467 --> 00:05:14.467
If I don't do it,
he's gonna be upset.

00:05:14.467 --> 00:05:16.601
-You have carrots.
You should eat vegetables.

00:05:16.601 --> 00:05:17.734
-Yeah.

00:05:17.734 --> 00:05:22.934
[ Laughter and applause ]

00:05:22.934 --> 00:05:24.300
-He's learning.

00:05:24.300 --> 00:05:28.434
♪♪

00:05:28.434 --> 00:05:31.434
-Um, did -- did all of this

00:05:31.434 --> 00:05:34.167
inspire your podcast,
"Longer Tables"?

00:05:34.167 --> 00:05:36.100
-Yeah.
You didn't do any "Mmm."

00:05:36.100 --> 00:05:37.701
[ Laughter ]
-The band was playing!

00:05:37.701 --> 00:05:39.334
-Ah, okay, okay.
Yeah.

00:05:39.334 --> 00:05:41.300
"Longer Tables"
is this very simple idea

00:05:41.300 --> 00:05:45.133
that if we build longer tables,
not higher walls,

00:05:45.133 --> 00:05:47.734
and we allow everybody
just to join the same table

00:05:47.734 --> 00:05:50.267
and give the same opportunity
to everybody,

00:05:50.267 --> 00:05:52.734
we can really build
a better world.

00:05:52.734 --> 00:05:54.167
That's the idea of
longer tables,

00:05:54.167 --> 00:05:58.601
where food is at the heart,
food is love, food is dignity.

00:05:58.601 --> 00:06:02.534
People, you know, don't want our
pity, they want our respect.

00:06:02.534 --> 00:06:04.801
Food is a great way
to give respect to people.

00:06:04.801 --> 00:06:07.334
-Yeah, the longer the table,
the more people can talk.

00:06:07.334 --> 00:06:09.200
[ Applause ]
Chef José Andrés.

00:06:09.200 --> 00:06:10.934
For more information on how
to get involved

00:06:10.934 --> 00:06:14.534
with the World Central Kitchen,
go to wck.org.

00:06:14.534 --> 00:06:16.367
We'll be right back
with Ana Fabrega.

00:06:16.367 --> 00:06:17.634
Stick around, everybody.

